Intuition
Since both arrays are already sorted, the most straightforward idea is to merge them into a single sorted array and then directly compute the median. This approach is easy to understand but requires extra space and takes linear time.

However, the problem asks for an algorithm with O(log(m + n)) time complexity. Instead of merging, we can partition the two arrays such that the left half contains exactly half of the elements and every element in the left partition is less than or equal to every element in the right partition. Using binary search on the smaller array allows us to find this partition efficiently.

Approach
Approach 1: Merge the Arrays
Use two pointers to merge both sorted arrays into a new sorted array.
If the total number of elements is odd, return the middle element.
If it is even, return the average of the two middle elements.
This approach is simple and intuitive but uses extra space.

Approach 2: Binary Search (Optimal)
Always perform binary search on the smaller array to reduce the search space.
Let cut1 be the partition in the first array and cut2 be the corresponding partition in the second array so that the left side always contains (m + n + 1) / 2 elements.
Find:
maxLeft1, minRight1
maxLeft2, minRight2
If
maxLeft1 <= minRight2
maxLeft2 <= minRight1
then the correct partition is found.
If the total number of elements is even, the median is the average of the maximum element on the left and the minimum element on the right.
Otherwise, the median is simply the maximum element on the left.
If the partition is invalid, move the binary search left or right accordingly.
Complexity
Approach 1 (Merge)
Time complexity: O(m + n)
Space complexity: O(m + n)
Approach 2 (Binary Search)
Time complexity: O(log(min(m, n)))
Space complexity: O(1)
Code
Approach 1 (Merge)
class Solution {
    public double findMedianSortedArrays(int[] nums1, int[] nums2) {
        int m = nums1.length;
        int n = nums2.length;

        double[] merged = new double[m + n];

        int i = 0, j = 0, k = 0;

        while (i < m && j < n) {
            if (nums1[i] <= nums2[j]) {
                merged[k++] = nums1[i++];
            } else {
                merged[k++] = nums2[j++];
            }
        }

        while (i < m) {
            merged[k++] = nums1[i++];
        }

        while (j < n) {
            merged[k++] = nums2[j++];
        }

        int len = merged.length;

        if (len % 2 == 1) {
            return merged[len / 2];
        }

        return (merged[len / 2 - 1] + merged[len / 2]) / 2.0;
    }
}
Approach 2 (Binary Search)
class Solution {
    public double findMedianSortedArrays(int[] nums1, int[] nums2) {

        if (nums1.length > nums2.length) {
            return findMedianSortedArrays(nums2, nums1);
        }

        int n = nums1.length;
        int m = nums2.length;
        int totalLength = n + m;

        int half = (totalLength + 1) / 2;
        int low = 0;
        int high = n;

        while (low <= high) {

            int cut1 = low + (high - low) / 2;
            int cut2 = half - cut1;

            int maxLeft1 = (cut1 == 0) ? Integer.MIN_VALUE : nums1[cut1 - 1];
            int minRight1 = (cut1 == n) ? Integer.MAX_VALUE : nums1[cut1];

            int maxLeft2 = (cut2 == 0) ? Integer.MIN_VALUE : nums2[cut2 - 1];
            int minRight2 = (cut2 == m) ? Integer.MAX_VALUE : nums2[cut2];

            if (maxLeft1 <= minRight2 && maxLeft2 <= minRight1) {

                if (totalLength % 2 == 0) {
                    return (Math.max(maxLeft1, maxLeft2) +
                            Math.min(minRight1, minRight2)) / 2.0;
                }

                return Math.max(maxLeft1, maxLeft2);
            }

            else if (maxLeft1 > minRight2) {
                high = cut1 - 1;
            }

            else {
                low = cut1 + 1;
            }
        }

        return 0.0;
    }
}
